      "type": "DroughtEvent",
      "label": "European drought",
      "is_main": true,
      "evidence": [
        "Human-caused climate change has intensified a drought across Europe, with extreme heat, rather than low rainfall, driving the exceptionally dry conditions, a team of international scientists said on Thursday.",
        "The continent is gripped by a major drought, with countries from France to Romania confronting parched rivers, water restrictions and devastating wildfires after a historic heatwave in June."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "EV2",
      "node_kind": "Event",
      "type": "StudyEvent",
      "label": "New study",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "A new study found that today's hotter climate has accelerated moisture loss from Europe's soils, lakes and rivers, increasing the likelihood of bone-dry conditions.",
        "For the study, scientists from Europe, the United States and the United Kingdom used weather data and computer models to compare a drought in today's hotter world with a climate that was 1.4C cooler."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "EV3",
      "node_kind": "Event",
      "type": "HeatwaveEvent",
      "label": "Historic June heatwave",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "Last month was the hottest June ever recorded in western Europe, the Copernicus Climate Change Service said, with a brutal heatwave causing thousands of excess deaths."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "EV4",
      "node_kind": "Event",
      "type": "WildfireEvent",
      "label": "Wildfires in Europe",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "This rapid drying also primed the land for wildfires, he added."
      ],
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "id": "CL1",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Drought driven by heat",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "Overall, our results show that this drought is not primarily a story of low rainfalls but of a warmer atmosphere that is causing more moisture deficits on the land.",
        "This means that even in regions where the rainfall has changed little or is projected to increase seasonally, drought risk continues to rise."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "CL2",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Warmer atmosphere holds more moisture",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "This is because a warmer atmosphere can hold about seven per cent more water vapour for every 1C rise in temperature, scientists say."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "CL3",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Climate change intensifies extremes",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "Scientists say climate change is making destructive events such as droughts, floods and heatwaves more frequent and intense, and that Europe is the world's fastest-warming continent."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "CL4",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Hot air dries soils",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "If you have increasingly hot air, it will soak the soils dry. And then it gets even hotter, and the soils get even drier, he said."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "CL5",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Drought more likely today",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "They found that extremely dry soils were five times more likely today in western Europe than in a world without climate change, and 11 times more likely in eastern Europe.",
        "The exceptionally high levels of evaporative demand – the atmosphere's potential to draw moisture from soils and plants – observed between April and June in western Europe were about 80 times more likely."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    },
    {
      "id": "CL6",
      "node_kind": "Claim",
      "type": "ScientificClaim",
      "label": "Thirstier atmosphere worsens drought",
      "is_main": false,
      "evidence": [
        "Because the atmosphere is now thirstier, the same rainfall deficit that might not have caused a drought in the pre-industrial climate can now lead to a more severe event, said Zachariah."
      ],
      "confidence": 1.0
    }
